courses.learning_outcomes:
After taking this course, students should be able to:

1. Understand the basic framework of Territorial Spatial Planning (TSP) in China;
2. Understand the basic planning standards and guidelines in planning practice in China;
3. Compare the Hong Kong planning system and practices with Chinese mainland;
4. Find planning information and keep track of the planning policy, latest planning issues in Chinese mainland and critically understand the gist of the policy and issues;
5. Build up basic professional skills in site and project analysis;
6. Build up multi-dimensional skills and confidence in planning communication.

By the end of the program, graduates will be able to:

1. **Address** planning matters subject to the social, physical, and economic development of urban and rural environments in Hong Kong, prioritizing the best interests of the community;
2. **Meet** the full membership (MHKIP) requirements of the Hong Kong Institute of Planners;
3. **Demonstrate awareness** of the role of town planning in strategic planning and planning & engineering (P&E) studies in both public and private sector contexts;
4. **Understand** the key tripartite relationship in the development process among town planning, lease control, and building planning regulations;
5. **Possess** the knowledge and skill set necessary to give employers confidence in their ability to practice town planning effectively, with particular reference to Hong Kong;
6. **Promote** cooperation and mutual understanding with related disciplines—such as traffic engineering, environmental management, and landscaping—within the development process in Hong Kong; and
7. **Maintain** a sustainable and professional attitude, applying good practices to enhance the professionalism of town planning.
